plementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${I}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0,$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =2\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$ So, we can apply the initial condition $I(0)=2,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(0) & =2 \\\\ A{e}^{-5(0)}+\\frac{2}{5} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $I(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5t}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $t=0.02$ seconds is\n\n$$\nI(0.02)=\\frac{2}{5}(4{e}^{-5(0.02)}+1)≈1.85\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486490 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186486509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486490 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7b1b56dd-b9e3-5d01-ac4e-820cdf14cd96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486490 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186120263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486501 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7db3e8f-89ed-5472-b6be-333d89d420fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486501 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186311743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486508 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186486501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486508 #edb/